Most Asked Interview Questions
Q1. Why is Vector slower than ArrayList?

Because every public method in Vector is synchronized, requiring lock acquisition and release even in single-threaded scenarios.

Q2. Why doesn't everyone use Vector?

It provides thread safety through coarse-grained synchronization, which reduces performance. In single-threaded applications it adds unnecessary overhead.

Q3. Why were Concurrent Collections introduced?

To provide thread-safe collections with better scalability and performance than legacy synchronized collections like Vector, Stack, and Hashtable.

Q4. Why use CopyOnWriteArrayList instead of Vector?

Because CopyOnWriteArrayList allows lock-free reads and is highly efficient for applications where reads greatly outnumber writes.

Q5. Why use ConcurrentHashMap instead of Hashtable?

Because ConcurrentHashMap allows much higher concurrency by avoiding a single global lock and using more fine-grained synchronization and CAS internally.

Q6. Why use ArrayDeque instead of Stack?

Because Stack extends the legacy Vector and synchronizes every operation. ArrayDeque is faster, more memory-efficient, and is the recommended stack implementation for single-threaded code